{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.interface_one_eq_loop_value","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.interface_one_eq_loop_value","kind":"theorem","line":225,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L225-L237","doc":"**`k = 1` is the member whose interface IS the loop value.** With\n`interface_reflection`'s fixed point that is two independent characterisations of\nthe same point — and `SymmetrySignAmGm.crossing_weight` shows it is exactly where\n`δ` vanishes. So the one member with an argument predicts NO symmetry energy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.interface_reflection","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.interface_reflection","kind":"theorem","line":208,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L208-L223","doc":"**The reflection.** `k ↦ 2 - k` inverts the interface about the loop value:\nthe product of the two is `1/[2]_q²`. This is why `δ` is odd about `k = 1`, and\nwhy `k = 1` — the only fixed point — is where it vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.interface_closed_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.interface_closed_form","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L188-L206","doc":"**The interface in closed form**, `f_pn(k) = [2]_{q^{2-k}}/([2]_q·[2]_{q^k})`,\nwith the like-species bonds pinned as `b_pp = q²/[2]_q`, `b_nn = q⁻²/[2]_q`.\nOne expression for the arithmetic (`k = 0`), geometric (`k = 1`) and harmonic\n(`k = 2`) means. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_eq_inv_rpow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_eq_inv_rpow","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L174-L186","doc":"**`w_k = 1/(q^{2k}+1)`.** The definition's `q^{-k}/[2]_{q^k}` is this in\ndisguise; with `b_pp/b_nn = q⁴` it reads `1/(1 + (b_pp/b_nn)^{k/2})`, so `k/2`\nis the power of the bond ratio the average is weighted by. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_strictAnti","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_strictAnti","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L105-L128","doc":"**`w_k` is strictly decreasing in `k`** for `q > 1`, so the family sweeps\nmonotonically from `1/2` downward. With `weight_one` this places every `k > 1`\nstrictly below the crossing — the region `SymmetrySignAmGm.delta_nonpos_iff`\nidentifies as the SEMF sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_one","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L94-L103","doc":"`w_1 = 1/(q²+1)`, which `SymmetrySignAmGm.crossing_weight` shows is exactly\nthe weight landing on the geometric mean `1/[2]_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.weight_zero","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L87-L92","doc":"`w_0 = 1/2`: the corpus's averaging rule is the undeformed member. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.bar_weight_bar_eq_one_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.bar_weight_bar_eq_one_sub","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L74-L85","doc":"**Every member of the family is bar-invariant.** Bar invariance of the\ninterface `w·b_pp + (1-w)·b_nn` requires exactly `w̄ = 1 - w`, and that holds for\nthe whole family — so the condition constrains one degree of freedom and leaves\n`k` free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.wbar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.wbar","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L71-L72","doc":"Its image under the bar involution `q ↦ 1/q`, written out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.w","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BarWeightFamily.w","kind":"def","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BarWeightFamily.lean#L67-L69","doc":"The bar-symmetric weight family, `w_k = q^{-k}/(q^k + q^{-k})`. At `k = 0`\nthis is `1/2`, the corpus's averaging rule. ","signatureChunk":0}]}